NNAAMMEE
     ppmmccccoonnttrrooll — control hardware performance monitoring counters

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ppmmccccoonnttrrooll [--cc _c_p_u | --dd _p_m_c | --ee _p_m_c] ...
     ppmmccccoonnttrrooll --ll
     ppmmccccoonnttrrooll --LL
     ppmmccccoonnttrrooll --ss

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ppmmccccoonnttrrooll utility controls the operation of the system's hardware
     performance monitoring counters.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
     The ppmmccccoonnttrrooll utility processes options in command line order, so later
     options modify the effect of earlier ones.  The following options are
     available:

     --cc _c_p_u  Subsequent enable and disable options affect the CPU denoted by
             argument _c_p_u.  The argument _c_p_u is a number denoting a CPU in the
             system, or “*”, denoting all unhalted CPUs in the system.

     --dd _p_m_c  Disable PMC number _p_m_c on the CPU specified by --cc, preventing it
             from being used till subsequently re-enabled.  The argument _p_m_c
             is a number denoting a specific PMC, or “*” denoting all the PMCs
             on the specified CPU.

             Only idle PMCs may be disabled.

     --ee _p_m_c  Enable PMC number _p_m_c, on the CPU specified by --cc, allowing it to
             be used in the future.  The argument _p_m_c is a number denoting a
             specific PMC, or “*” denoting all the PMCs on the specified CPU.
             If PMC _p_m_c is already enabled, this option has no effect.

     --ll      List available hardware performance counters and their current
             disposition.

     --LL      List available hardware performance counter classes and their
             supported event names.

     --ss      Print driver statistics maintained by hwpmc(4).

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     To disable all PMCs on all CPUs, use the command:
           pmccontrol -d*

     To enable all PMCs on all CPUs, use:
           pmccontrol -e*

     To disable PMCs 0 and 1 on CPU 2, use:
           pmccontrol -c2 -d0 -d1

     To disable PMC 0 of CPU 0 only, and enable all other PMCS on all other
     CPUs, use:
           pmccontrol -c* -e* -c0 -d0

DDIIAAGGNNOOSSTTIICCSS
     The ppmmccccoonnttrrooll utility exits 0 on success, and >0 if an error occurs.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     pmc(3), pmclog(3), hwpmc(4), pmcstat(8), sysctl(8)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The ppmmccccoonnttrrooll utility first appeared in FreeBSD 6.0.

